  "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=\u27e81,0,0\u27e9.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=\u27e80,1,-2\u27e9.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =\u27e81,0,0\u27e9\\times \u27e80,1,-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =\u27e80,2,1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $\u27e80,2,1\u27e9.$",
